 # Changing the queue's own schema while the queue keeps running
 
-This page is the procedure for one change: a column the queue's own table is gaining, while the
-queue is serving. It is written as phases because a single migration cannot be safe while two
-releases are running against the same database, and it is written down because the person who runs
-the middle one is not always the person who wrote the first.
-
-<!-- TODO: name the change (the table and the column), say what the value is and where it is
-computed, and say why the value already being in every row is what makes this change one nobody
-can get wrong. Then say who reads this page and when. -->
+This page is the procedure for one change: `jobs` gains `payload_bytes`, an integer derived from
+the payload the row already carries. It is written as three phases because a single migration
+cannot be safe while two releases are running against the same database, and it is written down
+here because the person who runs phase two is not always the person who wrote phase one.
+
+The whole thing is one value: `jobq::engine::Change`, in `src/engine/schema.rs`. The three phases
+are `Change::expand`, `Change::backfill` and `Change::contract`, and each of them takes what the
+database already answers — `is the column there`, `how many rows have no value`, `has the
+constraint been validated` — and answers what there is to do. Nothing here is measured with a
+clock: the budget in `course/tracks/jobq/budget/3.23-online-schema.toml` is written in counts, so
+the numbers below are the same on a loaded machine as on an idle one.
 
 ## Phase one — expand
 
 ```sql
-TODO
+alter table jobs add column payload_bytes integer;
+alter table jobs add constraint jobs_payload_bytes_known
+    check (payload_bytes is not null) not valid;
+create index jobs_unfilled_payload_bytes on jobs (id) where payload_bytes is null;
 ```
 
-<!-- TODO: the statements, in order, and why there are so few of them. Say which of them hold a
-lock and for how long, say why the column is nullable and has no default, and say what the release
-that is already running does when it writes a row — that release is the reason this phase exists.
-Then say what the read path answers while the column is empty, and why it is that expression rather
-than a second one written out. -->
+Three statements and no `update`. The column is **nullable and has no default**, which is the
+whole of what lets the release that is already running keep inserting rows that do not name it —
+and `add column … not null` would refuse exactly those inserts, on the third line of a deploy. The
+constraint is `not valid`, so it holds for every write from now on and tells the database to skip
+the rows that are already there: `migrations/0006_job_states.sql` added a state constraint
+*validating*, which is right for a table of ten rows and a full scan under a deploy's lock for a
+table that is the deployment's queue. The index is partial on the predicate the backfill reads, so
+finding what is left is a lookup rather than a scan.
+
+The read path answers `coalesce(payload_bytes, octet_length(payload::text))` — `Change::fallback`,
+built from `Change::computed` rather than written out a second time, because the number the
+backfill stores and the number a reader computes have to be one expression in two places. A reader
+that answered the column and nothing else answers `null` for every row that predates the change.
 
 ## Phase two — backfill
 
-<!-- TODO: say where the middle phase runs, and why that is the queue's own work rather than a
-script next to it. Then fill in the table below: one row per metric the harness prints, what it
-counts, the baseline `course/tracks/jobq/budget/3.23-online-schema.toml` records for it, and why it
-is a decision rather than a constant. The names are the ones `jobq::engine::PLAN_METRICS` lists, and
-the spec compares this page with that list. -->
+The middle phase is **jobs on a queue in the table being changed**: `Change::backfill` answers a
+plan of batches, and each batch is one `update … where payload_bytes is null … limit 256`. It runs
+on the same workers, through the same claims, with the same trail as the traffic it is walking
+past, and that is why the two numbers below are decisions rather than constants:
 
 | metric | what it counts | baseline | why |
 | --- | --- | --- | --- |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
-| `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` | `TODO` |
+| `backfill_jobs` | the jobs a plan for 4096 rows submits | 16 | 4096 rows at 256 a batch. One statement per batch is what makes a batch atomic: a batch that ran filled all of its rows and a batch that never ran filled none, so a run that was interrupted is a run anybody can finish. |
+| `burst_max` | the most batches due in one interval | ≤ 1 | the rate, as an upper bound: one batch a second, so the migration is a background trickle rather than a burst that consumes the queue's own capacity. A plan whose interval is zero is a *number* here — 16, against a limit of 1 — which is the defect this guard exists for. |
+| `resume_ratio_max` | jobs left ÷ jobs for the whole table | ≤ 0.6 | the plan is a function of what the catalog says is missing, not of the size of the table, so resuming from half the table is half the jobs. `1.0` says a run that was interrupted at the halfway mark plans to look at every row again. |
+
+Both guards are upper bounds rather than targets: a plan that submits fewer jobs, or reschedules
+them further apart, passes. A ratio with no denominator is not printed at all — the harness leaves
+the line out rather than reporting `0`, because a zero compares with the bound like any other ratio
+and would report a pass for a plan that measured nothing.
 
